e knight alone and play 22…Qxb3. White answers 23.Nxb3, taking the queen on b3 and threatening Rxa7, winning a pawn. Then 24.g4 threatens gxf5, winning a knight for a pawn, and 25.Rc6 threatens Rxd6, winning a knight. White ends up a pawn ahead: White holds the balance (+0.20)."},{"title":"Why not save the knight first?","text":"Saving it with 22.Ne4, the best way to do that, is clearly weaker (−5.50 against +0.20). Black answers 22…Qxd1, taking the queen on d1. That would leave White losing."}]},{"ply":56,"name":"28…Rc8","cls":"blunder","color":"b","headline":"28…Rc8 is a blunder: Black's winning chances go from 45% to 23%.","points":["White answers 29.Rxb5, which takes the pawn on b5.","After Rxb5, White wins 1 point of material within 2 moves.","Better was 28…Bxb2 (+0.55), which takes the pawn on b2."],"better":"28…Bxb2 takes the b2 pawn and leaves White with an isolated pawn on a2. After 29.Rxb5 Be5 30.Rb4 Rc8 31.Rbc4 Nd6 32.Bxd6 Rxc4 33.Nxc4 Bxd6 34.Nxd6 Rxd6, the rooks are traded and the bishops are traded (+0.55)."},{"ply":61,"name":"31.axb3","cls":"miss","color":"w","headline":"31.axb3 lets the win slip: 31.Nxb3 kept White winning. White's winning chances go from 73% to 62%.","points":["Black answers 31…Nd6: shores up the e4 pawn, which was under as much attack as it had defence (1 against 1).","Better was 31.Nxb3 (+2.76), which takes the pawn on b3."],"better":"31.Nxb3 wins a pawn and stops …bxc2. After 31…Ra8 32.Rb4 Ne7 33.a4 Nc6 34.Rbc4, White comes out a pawn up (+2.76)."},{"ply":88,"name":"44…Rb2","cls":"mistake","color":"b","headline":"44…Rb2 is a mistake: Black's winning chances go from 44% to 31%.","points":["White answers 45.Kf1.","Better was 44…Kh7 (+0.68)."],"better":"44…Kh7 prepares …exf3+ and …Kg7. Engine line: 45.f4 exf3+ 46.Kxf3 Kg7 47.Ke4 Rb5 (+0.68)."},{"ply":89,"name":"45.Kf1","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"45.Kf1! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["The next best move, 45.Kg1, would drop White's winning chances from 69% to 54% (+0.43).","45.Kf1 prepares Ke1 and Kd2.","The engine's plan continues with Ke1 and Kd2: this move is the first step.","Black answers 45…Kf6.","Engine line: 45…Kf6 46.Ke1 Rb1+ 47.Kd2 Rb5 48.Kc3 (+2.34)."]},{"ply":102,"name":"51…Kh7","cls":"blunder","color":"b","headline":"51…Kh7 is a blunder: Black's winning chances go from 34% to 7%.","points":["White answers 52.Kf6, which attacks the f7 pawn, which is undefended.","After Kf6, White wins 1 point of material within 4 moves.","Better was 51…Rb6 (+1.81)."],"better":"51…Rb6 prepares …Rb5 and …Rb1. After 52.Kxe4 Rb5 53.Kd4 Rb1 54.Kc4 Rb2, Black is a pawn down (+1.81)."},{"ply":103,"name":"52.Kf6","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"52.Kf6! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Attacks the f7 pawn, which is undefended.","The next best move, 52.Rf8, would drop White's winning chances from 93% to 74% (+2.84).","52.Kf6 attacks the f7 pawn, which is undefended.","Black answers 52…Rb2, attacking the f2 pawn, which is undefended, leaving White with an isolated pawn on e3.","After 52…Rb2 53.Kxf7 Rxf2+ 54.Ke8 Rb2 55.Rd8 Rxb7 56.Rd7+ Rxd7 57.Kxd7 Kg7, the rooks are traded and White is a pawn down (+7.32)."]}],"opening":{"name":"King's Indian Attack: Double Fianchetto","eco":"A07","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 4.Bg2: 7 moves in all, 4 by White and 3 by Black.","Theory runs out after 4.Bg2: too few games between strong players reached this position to go further."]}}